  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "not stated",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper discusses induction-head-style content matching and argues that repeated content can make occurrence matching ambiguous, motivating positional retrieval. It does not measure or intervene on induction heads themselves.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports exact-copy outcomes for synthetic transformer models and frontier LLMs. These measure copying, but not induction-head formation, composition, or activity, so the rows are linked to none of V1–V7. Figure 1 gives per-model accuracy curves without extractable numerical values; only the paper’s qualitative summary and the separate Table 1 examples are recorded.",
    "test_reason": "The paper cites induction heads (Olsson et al., 2022) only as motivation. It argues that content-based prefix matching becomes ambiguous under repetition, then studies positional encodings for exact copying. It never identifies, measures or intervenes on induction heads, previous-token heads, in-context-learning loss by token index, or their formation. Its argument concerns when content matching suffices for copying repeated strings. It does not claim any of V1–V7 is wrong, so T5 does not fit, and the study does not test the claim.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. Repetition makes content-based occurrence matching ambiguous. VO-rotation with prime periods and CRT lets a two-layer, low-precision model copy and terminate under arbitrary repetition. Eventually-constant bias-only models face a width-independent finite-precision obstruction. In experiments, trained VO models copy near-perfectly up to about 5000 and collapse beyond the period product 5005.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study investigates copying under repetition using theoretical positional constructions, finite-precision analysis, and synthetic copying experiments; it does not reproduce the original induction-head measurements or training-loss/ICL analysis.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Copying accuracy varies with repetition distribution, positional encoding, evaluation length, and, for some all-identical extrapolation results, training seed. VO-Rotation accuracy drops around and beyond the selected period product 5005.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Frontier LLMs and standard positional-encoding transformers copy random strings well but fail on long repeated or all-identical strings, overrunning or truncating. A value/output-rotation 2-layer model copies perfectly through 2000 and near-perfectly to about 5005.",
        "proposed_claim": "Content-based copying, such as induction-style prefix matching, fails to locate continuation and termination under long repetition; positional retrieval is needed.",
